1 CREATE

   Creates a text file from the lines of SYS$INPUT: at the terminal, the
   lines typed up to Ctrl/Z; in a command procedure, the data lines
   after the command, up to the next line starting with "$" (or the end
   of a DECK).  The file is made even when there are no lines.

   Format

     CREATE filespec

   CREATE has other forms, each a command of its own:

     CREATE/DIRECTORY    Creates a directory on a mounted volume.
     CREATE/FDL          Creates an empty file from an FDL file.
     CREATE/NAME_TABLE   Creates a logical name table.

2 Parameter

 filespec

   The file to create.  A file on a mounted volume, such as
   DUA0:[WORK]NOTES.TXT, or NOTES.TXT with the default directory on a
   volume, is made with variable-length records and carriage return
   carriage control, as VMS makes a text file, and is a new version if
   the file exists.

   A host file is a plain host text file, each line ending as the
   host's lines do: a line feed on Unix and macOS, a carriage return and
   line feed on Windows.  A name with a host path ("./notes.txt", quoted
   to keep its case) is a host file, and so is a bare name when the
   default directory isn't on a volume.

2 Qualifiers
/HOST

   After filespec, makes it a host file name, whatever it looks like:
   CREATE "notes.txt"/HOST.

/LOG

   Shows the file created, with its version.

2 Examples

   1.$ CREATE NOTES.TXT
     This is the first line.
     This is the last.
     ^Z

     At the terminal, makes NOTES.TXT from the two lines typed.

   2.$ CREATE/LOG DUA0:[WORK]SETUP.DAT
     ITEM ONE
     ITEM TWO
     $ TYPE DUA0:[WORK]SETUP.DAT

     In a command procedure, makes SETUP.DAT from the data lines after
     the command.

2 /DIRECTORY

   Creates a directory on a mounted volume, and each directory above it
   that doesn't exist yet: CREATE/DIRECTORY [A.B.C] makes whichever of
   [A], [A.B], and [A.B.C] are missing.  A directory that exists already
   is reported, and isn't an error.

   Format

     CREATE/DIRECTORY directory-spec[,...]

3 Parameter

 directory-spec[,...]

   A directory, such as [A.B] or DUA0:[A.B].  It may be relative to the
   default directory ([.SUB], [-.OTHER]; see SET DEFAULT), and its device
   may be a logical name.  It can't name a file or have wildcards.  With
   no device, and no default device on a mounted volume, the directory
   is made on the host, relative to the host's current directory.

3 Qualifiers
/ALLOCATION

      /ALLOCATION=n

   The blocks to give the directory at once.  The default is 1.

/LOG

   Shows the directory made.  The directories made above it aren't
   shown.

/OWNER_UIC

      /OWNER_UIC=option

   The directory's owner: a UIC, [group,member] in octal, or PARENT, the
   owner of the directory it is made in, which is the default.

/PROTECTION

      /PROTECTION=(category[:access][,...])

   The directory's protection: for each of SYSTEM, OWNER, GROUP, and
   WORLD (or S, O, G, and W), the access granted, any of R, W, E, and D,
   or none.  The default is the protection of the directory it is made
   in, less delete access; a category not given keeps that default.

/VERSION_LIMIT

      /VERSION_LIMIT=n

   The default version limit of files created in the directory, 0 for
   none.  The default is the limit of the directory it is made in.  A
   value out of range is reported and ignored, and the directory still
   made, as VMS does.

3 Examples

   1.$ CREATE/DIRECTORY [PROJECTS.TEST]

     Makes [PROJECTS] if it's missing, then [PROJECTS.TEST].

   2.$ CREATE/DIRECTORY/LOG/OWNER_UIC=[200,201]/VERSION_LIMIT=3 [USER]

     Makes [USER], owned by [200,201], keeping three versions of a file.

   3.$ CREATE/DIRECTORY/PROTECTION=(S:RWE,O:RWE,G:RE,W) [.SHARED]

     Makes [.SHARED] below the default directory, with no world access.

2 /FDL

   Creates an empty file from the description in an FDL (File
   Definition Language) file: sequential, relative, or indexed, with its
   record format, allocation, protection, dates, and an indexed file's
   keys and areas.  RMS makes the file, so what RMS refuses is reported
   with RMS's message.

   Format

     CREATE/FDL=fdl-filespec [filespec]

3 Parameter

 filespec

   The file to create.  Without one, the FDL file's FILE NAME names it.
   FILE DEFAULT_NAME gives what the name leaves out.  An existing file
   gets a new version.

3 Description

   An FDL file holds sections, such as FILE, RECORD, AREA n, and KEY n,
   each followed by its attributes, one to a line or separated by
   semicolons; "!" begins a comment.  Names may be abbreviated.  A
   warning leaves that attribute at its default, and the file is still
   made; an error means no file.  Indexed files are Prolog 3 only: PROLOG
   1 or 2, or a primary key whose segments overlap, is RMS$_PLV.

3 Qualifiers
/FDL

      /FDL=fdl-filespec

   The FDL file.  Its default type is .FDL.

/LOG

   Shows the file created, with its version.

3 Examples

   1.$ CREATE PARTS.FDL
     FILE
         ORGANIZATION indexed
     RECORD
         SIZE 80
     KEY 0
         SEG0_LENGTH 8
     KEY 1
         SEG0_LENGTH 20
         SEG0_POSITION 8
     $ CREATE/FDL=PARTS/LOG PARTS.IDX
     %FDL-I-CREATED, DUA1:[000000]PARTS.IDX;1 created

     Makes an indexed file with two keys.

2 /NAME_TABLE

   Creates a logical name table.

   Format

     CREATE/NAME_TABLE table-name

3 Parameter

 table-name

   The table's name, such as TAX.  To have file names looked up in the
   new table, add it to LNM$PROCESS (see the example).

3 Qualifiers
/EXECUTIVE_MODE

   Makes the table an executive-mode table.

/LOG

      /[NO]LOG

   /LOG (the default) reports an existing table superseded.

/PARENT_TABLE

      /PARENT_TABLE=table

   The new table's parent.  The default, LNM$PROCESS_DIRECTORY, makes a
   table private to the process; a shareable parent such as
   LNM$SYSTEM_DIRECTORY makes a shareable table.

/SUPERVISOR_MODE

   Makes the table a supervisor-mode table.  This is the default.

/USER_MODE

   Makes the table a user-mode table.

3 Examples

   1.$ CREATE/NAME_TABLE TAX
     $ DEFINE/TABLE=LNM$PROCESS_DIRECTORY LNM$PROCESS -
     _$ LNM$PROCESS_TABLE, TAX

     Makes the table TAX, and has file names looked up in it after the
     process table.
